  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 108 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nHeat through the Holiday Weekend:\n\nEnsembles exhibit strong agreement in maintaining the ridge--\nand even amplifying it across the central CONUS--for the end of\nthe week and through the weekend. Global models depict 850 mb\ntemperatures of 22-25 C, correlating to surface temperatures in\nthe upper 90s to near 100 degrees under mostly clear skies. Dew\npoint variance amongst the guidance is a bit larger owing to \ntypical biases (NAM too moist, GFS too dry, etc), but taking a \nrough mean of these values would put afternoon dewpoints in the \nmid 60s. Thus, maximum heat index values in the low to mid 100s \nwill continue to be common through at least Saturday. \nFurthermore, the NWS HeatRisk depicts widespread Major (Level 3 \nof 4) to Extreme (Level 4 of 4) values across southwest Missouri\nand southeast Kansas. Extreme HeatRisk signifies a long \nduration heat with little to no overnight relief. This heat \naffects anyone without effective cooling and/or adequate \nhydration; those with outdoor plans for the holiday in \nparticular should make cooling and hydration options readily \navailable. The Heat Advisory remains in effect areawide through \nSaturday. Further extensions may be necessary in future forecast\nupdates. \n\nLimited Rain Chances:\n\nThe ridge and weak upper-level flow will largely keep rain\nchances to a minimum through the long term period. Analysis of\nensemble postage stamps depicts a limited number of members\nproducing scattered QPF footprints somewhere across the region\nSaturday through Monday as a diffuse cold front dips into\nMissouri. A lack of strong forcing limits confidence in this QPF\nbeing realized, however, and NBM PoPs remain limited to 10-20%.\n\nEnsemble clusters diverge more significantly by the middle of\nnext week. There is some signal, at least, of a flattening \nridge and an open door for rain chances. NBM PoPs are currently \nat 20-30% on Wednesday and Thursday. With the persistent heat \nand a lack of appreciable rainfall in the meantime, drought \nconditions will deteriorate, and fuels will be more susceptible \nto fire starts. These conditions will need to be monitored \nclosely in the coming weeks for the potential of worsening \nimpacts.",
